      m('Know the startup cost versus the recurring monthly cost', ['cost','pricing'], 'TRT cost usually has at least two layers: getting started and staying on treatment. Compare both before you decide whether the program is actually sustainable.', ['Ask about consultation, onboarding, and baseline lab cost', 'Ask about the true monthly recurring total', 'Ask whether dose changes change the cost'], ['Only one number is quoted with no breakdown']),
      m('Labs, medication, and supplies may or may not be included', ['labs','medication','supply'], 'A plan that excludes labs, medications, or supplies can look cheap while costing more over time. Make the clinic explain what the quoted number really covers.', ['Ask which labs are included', 'Ask whether medication and syringes are included', 'Ask whether repeat labs cost extra'], ['No one can explain included vs extra items']),
      m('Insurance questions deserve plain-English answers', ['insurance','coverage'], 'Insurance coverage for TRT varies by the provider model, lab billing, and medication path. Ask specifically what they expect insurance to cover and what they do not.', ['Ask whether the clinic bills insurance directly', 'Ask whether meds are cash-pay only', 'Ask whether outside labs or pharmacies are allowed'], ['They say “insurance may help” with no specifics']),
      m('Use total cost to compare clinic quality, not just affordability', ['compare'], 'Price alone does not tell you whether a clinic is safe or useful. A higher fee may be reasonable if monitoring and follow-up are materially better; a lower fee is not a win if the process is sloppy.', ['Compare monitoring with the price', 'Compare access to the prescriber', 'Compare refill and follow-up reliability'], ['The clinic sells only on price and speed'])
